# Code owners for the module library.
#
# These paths decide what a module may do and who a village trusts. Contract
# clause 13 makes a human security review required before merge rather than
# optional after it, and this file is what routes the review to a human.
#
# BRANCH PROTECTION: leave "Require review from Code Owners" OFF until a second
# maintainer exists below.
#
# With one owner, the setting blocks every landing on that one person, and it
# blocks it hardest in the case that matters: an author cannot approve their own
# pull request, so any change to these paths opened by the sole owner can never
# be approved. A rule that stops the only maintainer from merging is not a
# security control, it is an outage. Until a second name is here, the review is
# a commitment and CODEOWNERS is what makes the request automatic.
#
# TO ADD A SECOND MAINTAINER:
#   1. Give them write access to the repository.
#   2. Uncomment the second name on each line below and replace the placeholder.
#   3. Only then turn on "Require review from Code Owners" in branch protection.
#      From that point every listing needs an approval from somebody who did not
#      write it, which is the property this file is really for.

# The registry itself: what a module is allowed to declare.
/shared/modules.ts            @Rieki777    # @second-maintainer

# The one capability gate. A change here changes what every member may do.
/shared/capabilities.ts       @Rieki777    # @second-maintainer

# Draft kinds carry the consequence lines a member reads before acting.
/shared/draftKinds.ts         @Rieki777    # @second-maintainer

# Module mounting and lifecycle.
/server/lib/modules.ts        @Rieki777    # @second-maintainer

# Integration secrets: write-only, read back masked. The credential plane is the
# mechanical definition of a tier, so this file is where a tier can quietly stop
# being honest.
/server/lib/secrets.ts        @Rieki777    # @second-maintainer

# The listing lint and the facts command. A change here changes what the
# automated half of intake checks, so it needs the same eyes as the rules
# themselves.
/scripts/validate-module.mjs  @Rieki777    # @second-maintainer
/scripts/module-facts.mjs     @Rieki777    # @second-maintainer
/scripts/check-doc-links.mjs  @Rieki777    # @second-maintainer

# The contract and everything a builder is told.
/docs/MODULE_LIBRARY_CONTRACT.md  @Rieki777    # @second-maintainer
/docs/modules/                    @Rieki777    # @second-maintainer

# The intake machinery. A pull request that edits the gate it must pass is the
# oldest trick there is.
/.github/workflows/ci.yml                  @Rieki777    # @second-maintainer
/.github/workflows/module-intake.yml       @Rieki777    # @second-maintainer
/.github/workflows/module-review-agent.yml @Rieki777    # @second-maintainer
/.github/review-agent/                     @Rieki777    # @second-maintainer
/.github/PULL_REQUEST_TEMPLATE/            @Rieki777    # @second-maintainer
/.github/CODEOWNERS                        @Rieki777    # @second-maintainer
